  "summary": "I reconciled Walter Kim's (pt-0005) outpatient medication list against the St. Brigid discharge summary (10/19) and its signed addendum, following RBC-TOC-02. Two medicines were changed. Lisinopril and spironolactone were not started or resumed because his potassium is high. One medicine needs your decision.\n\n**Changed**\n- **Furosemide:** increased from 20 mg to 40 mg daily, as the discharge summary says. The old order was replaced with a 40 mg tablet order (mr-t0001).\n- **Potassium chloride:** stopped, as the discharge summary says.\n\n**Held back because of potassium (RBC-TOC-02 §7)**\nHis potassium on the outside lab drawn 10/21 was 5.4 mmol/L (high); creatinine was 1.32.\n- **Lisinopril:** still on hold. The discharge condition for restarting it (creatinine below 1.5) is met, but the clinic rule doesn't allow restarting it while potassium is above 5.0.\n- **Spironolactone:** not ordered. The addendum changed the dose from 12.5 mg to 25 mg daily.\n- I created a task for you (task-t0003), due 10/26, to check a repeat basic metabolic panel and decide on both drugs. The discharge plan's metabolic panel 1 week after starting spironolactone doesn't apply yet, because it hasn't been started.\n\n**No change**\n- Metoprolol XL 50 mg is another name for his existing metoprolol succinate 50 mg order.\n- Atorvastatin and aspirin continue as before.\n- Pantoprazole was a hospital formulary swap, so he stays on his home omeprazole 20 mg and pantoprazole was not ordered.\n\n**Decision needed:** The discharge documents don't mention his metoprolol tartrate 25 mg twice daily. I left that order unchanged and sent you a message (comm-t0004). Together with the metoprolol succinate, he has two beta-blocker orders, so please decide whether the tartrate should continue.\n\nI documented all of this in a progress note (doc-t0005), citing the sources and the policy.",
